        <h2>Why small conversations matter</h2>

        <p>Small talk gets a bad name, but it does more than fill silences. In a well-known study, the psychologists Gillian Sandstrom and Elizabeth Dunn found that people felt happier on days when they had more brief, friendly exchanges with acquaintances: the people we only half know.</p>

        <p>For English learners, those short exchanges are also some of the best speaking practice there is. They're low stakes, they happen often, and they use the language people need most: greetings, quick questions, reactions, a bit of humour.</p>

        <h2>Why speaking practice works</h2>

        <p>The linguist Merrill Swain's "output hypothesis" explains part of it. When you have to say something rather than just understand it, you notice the gaps: the word you can't find, the tense you're unsure of. Noticing a gap is the first step to closing it.</p>

        <p>That's why we build short, everyday conversations into lessons rather than leaving speaking until the end. A two-minute chat about someone's weekend tests more of their English than a page of exercises.</p>

        <h2>From practice to confidence</h2>

        <p>Confidence comes from repetition. The more often you get through a conversation in English, even a short one, the less effort the next one takes. Gradually English stops feeling like a school subject and starts feeling like something you use.</p>

        <h2>Beyond the classroom</h2>

        <p>On the Dublin programme, English is the language of the whole day: in class, at meals, on excursions and in the evenings, with students from all over Europe. Every one of those small exchanges is practice, and none of it feels like homework.</p>

